  urldate = {2020-01-08},
  abstract = {Efficiently communicating information on vaccination is crucial to maintaining a high level of immunization coverage, but it implies finding the right content for the right audience. Most studies have tested the direct effects of messages on either anti-vaccine or vaccine-hesitant individuals. Pro-vaccination individuals, who represent the majority of the population and have been neglected in the literature, could play an important role relaying pro-vaccination messages through informal discussions. A pro-vaccination message is useful if provaccination individuals (i) find it plausible, (ii) remember it, and (iii) are willing to transmit it. Studies in cognitive psychology and cultural evolution have found that the framing of messages can affect these different steps.},
