  abstract = {The dominant focus on structured variation in parents' actions across vertically defined social classes (from the inequality or the culture-of-poverty perspective) continues to eclipse recent work on between-family variability within poor communities. Yet new research is emphasizing the variety of cultural models, kin supports, and parenting practices that circulate in low-income neighborhoods. This article details how 14 working-poor mothers act from a variety of cultural models or tacit pathways pertaining to one basic element of inner-city life: "choosing" an acceptable child care arrangement. It summarizes the surface-level paths that the women followed over a three-year period, including sources that informed their initial choice of child care, reactions to their care providers, and reasons for withdrawing from the arrangements. It then discusses how alternative cultural models, which vary by ethnic group, guided the decisions or drifts of these working-poor women as they moved their children into day care, returned to work, and pulled away from welfare.},
